              title: "Stop apologising for existing",
              source: "Opinion column in a London magazine (adapted)",
              text: "I am tired of clever people beginning every email with sorry. Sorry to bother you. Sorry for the delayed reply. Sorry if this is a silly question. It is not manners. It is a [[social convention|convención social]] that trains the writer to take up less room, and it is doing something to the word we actually need when we have caused harm.<br/><br/>I must admit I do it. If I had kept a folder, it would be longer than the Bangalore one, and most of the sentences in it would be apologies for existing. That is not the same as an apology for a harsh line. One of them repairs a wound. The other asks permission to have a pulse.<br/><br/>There is a cost. When you have already said sorry fourteen times that week, the fifteenth — the one that ought to cost you something — sounds like the rest. The colleague who received a rude paragraph on Tuesday hears, on Wednesday, the same music you used to ask for a file. In hindsight, you have spent the word. Even so, the habit is hard to break, because it is rewarded: people call you nice, and niceness is a currency that still spends.<br/><br/>I am not arguing for rudeness. I am arguing for keeping sorry for when you have something to own. You were late; say so, and say what you will do. You sent a tone you would not have sent if you had waited; name the tone, take responsibility, stop. You are not late, and you have not sent a tone: do not open with sorry. Open with the request. The request is allowed to take up the first line. That, too, is manners — the kind that leaves some sorry in the cupboard for the day you will need it.<br/><br/>A friend of mine, who is kinder than I am, says this is easy to say and ugly to do if you are the person in the room with less power. She is right. I would not tell a new barista to start a complaint to her manager without a softener. I would tell her not to spend her whole stock of sorry on the coffee machine being noisy. Save one. You are going to need it for a sentence that actually did some damage, and when that day comes you will want the word to still mean something.<br/><br/>On reflection, the Bangalore process and this column want the same thing from opposite doors. She wants a repair that works. I want a language in which a repair can still be heard. If we had not cheapened the word, we would not need a four-step method to make it land. We did. So we do.<br/><br/>I will add a practical test, because columns that only scold are another way of spending sorry. Tomorrow, before you send the first email of the day, count the sorrys already in the draft. If the first one is not attached to a harm you can name — a delay, a tone, a missed shift — take it out. Put the request in the first line. If the draft feels naked, that is the point: the request was allowed to take up that room all along. Keep one sorry for the afternoon you will actually need it. You will know the afternoon, because the wording will have been off, and you will want the word to still cost you something. That cost is the whole argument. A language in which every request arrives wrapped in sorry is a language in which a real apology cannot be heard. Save the word. Spend it once, on the sentence that did the damage, and then stop.",
